<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Saga Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1607 CE – 1871 CE · capital Saga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Nabeshima domain of Hizen, home of Arita porcelain and of the samurai classic Hagakure, and a pioneer of Western industrial technology.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-naoshige">Nabeshima Naoshige</a>, chief retainer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryuzoji-clan">Ryuzoji</a>, took over their domain after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryuzoji-takanobu">Ryuzoji Takanobu's</a> death in 1584 and was confirmed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>. After the last Ryuzoji heir died in 1607, Naoshige's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-katsushige">Katsushige</a> became lord in name as well as fact, with 357,000 koku.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Korean potters brought back from the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> began making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arita">Arita</a> around 1616, and the domain controlled its export through the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imari">Imari</a>, while the Nabeshima kilns made pieces for gifts to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>. Saga shared guard duty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagakure">Hagakure</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> treatise dictated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamamoto-tsunetomo">Yamamoto Tsunetomo</a>, was compiled here. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-naomasa">Nabeshima Naomasa</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan's</a> first reverberatory furnace in 1850.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Saga's modern weapons helped the imperial side in 1868. It became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saga-prefecture">Saga Prefecture</a> in 1871, and in 1874 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eto-shinpei">Eto Shinpei</a> led the failed Saga Rebellion.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/saga-domain.jpg" alt="Saga Domain" width="320" height="249"><figcaption>Image: Barfodt · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Nabeshima_Lords_of_Saga_in_Hizen_Province_(A).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1607 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saga</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nabeshima Naoshige, Nabeshima Katsushige, Nabeshima Naomasa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hizen Domain, Nabeshima Domain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1739&amp;focus=saga-domain" aria-label="Saga Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8013 970 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Saga Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8333 1226L8333 1226M8333 1226L8530 1145M8333 1226L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8333 1226h0M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8333 1226h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8333 1226h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Saga. <a href="/atlas?year=1739&amp;focus=saga-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
